# UI Safe Area

Every player-facing screen keeps interactive content inside the device safe area. The safe-area adjustment is one dedicated component on one prefab child — never per-screen magic offsets.

## The layout contract

`Canvas → [full-bleed background] + [SafeAreaRoot → interactive content]`

- **Interactive content** (buttons, text, panels, edge-anchored HUD chrome) goes **inside** the safe-area root.
- **Full-bleed visuals** (dim/blur backdrops, full-screen modal scrims, decorative effects) stay on the parent **before** the safe-area child, so they still cover notches and the home indicator. Never wrap a full-screen scrim in the safe area — it would letterbox under the notch.
- The safe-area root is a full-stretch `RectTransform` whose `anchorMin`/`anchorMax` are driven from the device insets by **one** safe-area component. That component is the only runtime code allowed to write those anchors (see the no-code-built-ui rule).

```csharp
// On the screen prefab (Inspector wiring):
// Canvas
//   ├── Backdrop          (full-bleed dim/blur — outside safe area)
//   └── SafeAreaRoot      (child carrying the project's safe-area component)
//         └── ContentRoot ([SerializeField] _contentRoot — buttons, text, panels)

[SerializeField] private RectTransform _contentRoot; // safe-area child; hierarchy authored on prefab
```

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — per-screen inset math in a view
_panel.offsetMax = new Vector2(0f, -NotchHeight);

// ✅ GOOD — view knows nothing about insets; the safe-area root above it handles them
_titleText.text = title;
```

## WebGL: the browser bridge

`Screen.safeArea` is **not implemented on the Web platform** — it always returns the full canvas. The safe-area component must read the browser's CSS `env(safe-area-inset-*)` values through a `.jslib` bridge (`[DllImport("__Internal")]`). This requires **`viewport-fit=cover`** in the hosting page's viewport meta tag, otherwise the insets are always 0. One bridge per project, shipped next to the component — not one per screen.

## Rules

- New screens never anchor interactive elements directly to the raw canvas — always through a safe-area root.
- The safe-area component may poll `Screen.safeArea` / the bridge in `Update` (no Unity safe-area-changed event exists) but must allocate nothing and only write anchors when the insets change.
- Dev/debug overlays are exempt.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] New screen prefab has a safe-area root; interactive content is under it, full-bleed visuals are not
- [ ] No per-screen inset offsets or notch constants in view code
- [ ] WebGL insets come from the shared jslib bridge; hosting page has `viewport-fit=cover`
- [ ] Safe-area component is the only code writing the root's anchors
